{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/154820-storyteller-how-do-you-consider-attributes-under-one-dot","id":154820,"title":"StoryTeller: How do you consider Attributes under one dot?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-05-31T07:41:50-07:00","id":154820,"title":"StoryTeller: How do you consider Attributes under one dot?","content":"I'm putting this under Off-Topic as it is a general question not limited to a particular game or version.<br /> <br /> How do you consider Attributes under one dot, if at all?<br /> <br /> Now, I have never got around to actually play a game with the ST system. So how things actually plays out in reality, instead of a mental exercise and all that... But... <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/confused.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Confused\" smilieid=\"10\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> As most (all?) of the ST games put 2 as human average and 5 as the peak of human performance, I personally regard one as far from hopeless. As I see it, Strength of one is your average office worker. Intelligence of one is quite an airhead/bubble-head. But they are far from &quot;hopeless&quot;. Getting below one is just a matter of getting into some form of &quot;impaired&quot; area, which would make the character hard to play in most games; but still able to attempt a skill using that attribute. A person with shakes and involuntary twitches would be in the below one range for Dexterity. But someone paralyzed from the neck down wouldn't even have a negative number.<br /> <br /> And as I feel to it id needed to be clarified after my involvement in the other thread...<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lundgren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/154820-storyteller-how-do-you-consider-attributes-under-one-dot?p=155941#post155941\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just to clarify. I'm not looking for what the rulebook says. I want to hear your own opinion on the topic. To broaden the topic a bit, I'm also curious on what an attribute value of one represent to you. If you like how the rules work, please explain with your own words rather than point to the rules.<br /> <br /> If you state one way is right, and everything else is wrong, without adding the qualifier &quot;for me&quot;, I might start to argue even if your way of right happens to correspond with my own preference.<br /> <br /> But I am not here to argue, I'm here to to share thoughts and read about others thoughts. Others thoughts, especially when they don't match my own, give me a possibility to put my own thoughts in perspective and analyze them. It also gives me a heads up on what areas I need clarify, or have clarified, when I play with a new group so people don't have different expectations. Discussing back and forth gives me an option to put my thoughts into words. Then, once in a while, someone mentions something that never have occurred to me before, that rare gem that forces me to reevaluate my whole thought on a topic.<br /> <br /> That is the purpose of this thread. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2839,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2839-lundgren","user":"Lundgren","date":". 06-01-2014, 05:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2839-lundgren","name":"Lundgren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-31T08:25:31-07:00","id":154842,"title":"","content":"I've never played a game of Exalted or World of Darkness where the possibility of somebody's Attribute dropping to zero was even a consideration. There are circumstances that can inflict this, but in my experience, they're not at all common.<br /> <br /> When the systems address the matter, the most common judgment seems to be that having no dots in an Attribute is crippling to the degree of invalidating any meaningful actions attached to the Attribute, and often with the Physical Attributes, it's outright fatal. But these rulings are usually attached to specific supernatural powers capable of inflicting the condition, rather than categorical statements on the consequences of having no dots in an Attribute.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-31T09:22:30-07:00","id":154894,"title":"","content":"Generally that. If an attribute drops to zero the character is incapacitated. If it's permanent, the person is likely either dead or if fortune smiles on them being cared for as an invalid by someone who cares for them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-01T10:46:45-07:00","id":155749,"title":"","content":"What Odd_Canuck said is true, but additionally, what you are thinking of as a rating of 1 is actually more like a 2, Lundgren. Even an average office worker occasionally applies their Strength enough to qualify for a 2. A zero in an attribute just takes you out of the running for using it at all. And no WoD system has never used negative numbers for anything, to my knowledge.<br /> <br /> In the original nWoD system, being physically or mentally handicapped would likely be handled by a flaw. In the updated God-Machine rules, such things would be covered by a Condition. Storytellers could also impose trait maximums until the flaw/Condition is resolved.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kismet's <a href=\"http://www.wod.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">WOD</a> - <a href=\"http://www.dnd.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">D&amp;D</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BoundinDreams\" target=\"_blank\">C:tL</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TheNamelessEmpire\" target=\"_blank\">M:tC</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","name":"KismetRose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-01T11:01:37-07:00","id":155756,"title":"","content":"It depends on the attribute, but in general it means that the character is incapable of using that attribute in any meaningful way and is, thus, crippled. I treat Str and Dex of 0 as the character being physically unable to move. They may be able to speak, or move their eyes, if I'm feeling generous. Sta 0 usually indicates a comatose state at best, or outright death at worst. Cha or Man of 0 means that the character is unable to communicate in any meaningful way. Something is wrong inside their head rendering them unable to articulate speach or sign language. This could manifest as some kind of &quot;locked in syndrome,&quot; or in less severe cases as a powerful aphasia. Appearance 0 is a special case- the character still has all their faculties, they are just so hiddeously ugly that it is questionable whether they can pass for human anymore. Perception 0 represents complete loss of the senses. The character is still conscious, but cannot experience their surroundings in any way. Kind of a variation on locked in syndrome. Int or Wits 0 represents a character that cannot think- essentially, a braindead vegetable. Perhaps their autonomic functions keep them alive, or perhaps they have to be on life support.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1416-nutiketaiel","name":"NutiketAiel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1416_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"303"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-01T12:36:09-07:00","id":155841,"title":"","content":"Thank you for your answers everyone <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>KismetRose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/154820-storyteller-how-do-you-consider-attributes-under-one-dot?p=155749#post155749\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What Odd_Canuck said is true, but additionally, what you are thinking of as a rating of 1 is actually more like a 2, Lundgren. Even an average office worker occasionally applies their Strength enough to qualify for a 2. A zero in an attribute just takes you out of the running for using it at all. And no WoD system has never used negative numbers for anything, to my knowledge.<br /> <br /> In the original nWoD system, being physically or mentally handicapped would likely be handled by a flaw. In the updated God-Machine rules, such things would be covered by a Condition. Storytellers could also impose trait maximums until the flaw/Condition is resolved.</div> </div> </div> </div>Over here in Sweden, we had a conscript military up to just recently, and they kept the statistics. The physical capacity of the conscripts have been in decline since the fifties, to really taking a dive the last couple of decades. So unless the two is a value relative to the current culture, the average person won't qualify for the average value of two.<br /> <br /> If I would try to translate myself into a character for the ST system, I would sat that my Strength is a solid 1 being your average office worker that haven't been working out for a decade or two. Back in my late teens when I spent 4 evenings each week in a dojo, I would say I had a Strength of 2. When it comes to craftsmen I met (welding, hardware shops, constructions, etc), I would put most of them at Strength of two and some on three.<br /> <br /> There was another thread on the topic started in the WoD forum just a couple of hours before I started this one, so as I found it I wrote a bit about in there. Personally, I find allowing attributes to still be usable below one to better fit my mental model, and it gives me a larger range of options if I chose to use it. So I just have to house rule it in.<br /> <br /> But I'm still interested in how other people perceive values below one. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2839-lundgren","name":"Lundgren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-01T13:22:18-07:00","id":155867,"title":"","content":"When 2 is defined as the average, you don't really get to say that below average is the average. You also need to leave some room for difference between your office workers and, say, cancer patients who lack the strength to walk up stairs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/271-jimb","name":"JimB","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar271_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-01T13:41:07-07:00","id":155886,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JimB</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/154820-storyteller-how-do-you-consider-attributes-under-one-dot?p=155867#post155867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When 2 is defined as the average, you don't really get to say that below average is the average. You also need to leave some room for difference between your office workers and, say, cancer patients who lack the strength to walk up stairs.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is what I feel I better do with allowing the attribute value to go under one while still being functional. As I otherwise feel that an Strength of two is different from culture to culture. Personally, I just rather say it is the average during human history; and at least I am quite a bit below that average.<br /> <br /> Then I can model that cancer patient as having a value of -1. If that person was a former Olympian wrestler, having four dots in the skill, he would still be able to apply three dices against the office worker that did MA in his teens (say two dices, one from the attribute and one from the skill). The wrestler might run out of steam a lot faster, but might be able to choke out the office worker before that, by just being a lot more experienced.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2839-lundgren","name":"Lundgren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-01T14:12:48-07:00","id":155941,"title":"","content":"Just to clarify. I'm not looking for what the rulebook says. I want to hear your own opinion on the topic. To broaden the topic a bit, I'm also curious on what an attribute value of one represent to you. If you like how the rules work, please explain with your own words rather than point to the rules.<br /> <br /> If you state one way is right, and everything else is wrong, without adding the qualifier &quot;for me&quot;, I might start to argue even if your way of right happens to correspond with my own preference.<br /> <br /> But I am not here to argue, I'm here to to share thoughts and read about others thoughts. Others thoughts, especially when they don't match my own, give me a possibility to put my own thoughts in perspective and analyze them. It also gives me a heads up on what areas I need clarify, or have clarified, when I play with a new group so people don't have different expectations. Discussing back and forth gives me an option to put my thoughts into words. Then, once in a while, someone mentions something that never have occurred to me before, that rare gem that forces me to reevaluate my whole thought on a topic.<br /> <br /> That is the purpose of this thread. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2839-lundgren","name":"Lundgren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-01T20:39:44-07:00","id":156211,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lundgren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/154820-storyteller-how-do-you-consider-attributes-under-one-dot?p=155841#post155841\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Over here in Sweden, we had a conscript military up to just recently, and they kept the statistics. The physical capacity of the conscripts have been in decline since the fifties, to really taking a dive the last couple of decades. So <b>unless the two is a value relative to the current culture,</b> the average person won't qualify for the average value of two.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this is the primary point of divergence I'd have with your perspective on how to use the dot scales. Context rules all; if you're playing a game, say, with a special United Nations task force in a war-torn area, and you've got your average Swede in the mix, and you want to go by these statistics, then sure, that might be a good reason to give that character a one dot score, the below-average/deficient rating in an attribute.<br /> <br /> But if you're playing in a small town in Sweden, or wherever it might be, sticking to this representation is a concession to trying to simulate the world relatively strictly, and that's not one of the various Storytellering Systems' strengths. (Well, an Exalted mortals game might benefit from that some; again, context is key.) The games start you with a set dot spread not really because everybody in the world conforms to that level of aptitude, but because the developers felt that created about enough room to create interesting characters with differing fields of specialty at about a level appropriate to engage with the game at the base level. The kind of thinking that would give most Swedes, say, a Strength of 1 would homogenize your characters without really saying much interesting about the game at hand, unless your Vampire game is about declining health statistics.<br /> <br /> Or in other words, two isn't a value relative to the current culture, it's a value relative to the <i>context of the game,</i> whether that's a sleepy New England town or jet-setting across Europe. If your mummy frankensteins aren't visiting Moscow then it doesn't really matter to accurately model whether they would be able to arm-wrestle your average Muscovite. Five dots is a simple spread that's versatile without requiring much thinking. Apply the scale to your game and not your game to the scale.<br /> <br /> (There are kludges, like some games which assign weight levels to what a particular Strength rating can lift. Consider this a concession to needing some measure just to keep play going; it'd take a lot of work to overhaul White Wolf games to match this level of detail in all respects, and less work just to accept that things like weight lifting values are flattened out and handwaved a little for the sake of the game.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-01T21:23:04-07:00","id":156238,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lundgren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/154820-storyteller-how-do-you-consider-attributes-under-one-dot?p=155941#post155941\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just to clarify. I'm not looking for what the rulebook says. I want to hear your own opinion on the topic.</div> </div> </div> </div>My own opinion is I'll stick with what the book says, since that doesn't put me in a position where I have to invent new rules that broaden a 1-5 scale to include negative integers in order to work. That may sound like a cop-out, but you did ask.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lundgren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/154820-storyteller-how-do-you-consider-attributes-under-one-dot?p=155941#post155941\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To broaden the topic a bit, I'm also curious on what an Attribute value of one represent to you.</div> </div> </div> </div>Below average; deficient in daily situations such that special allowances need to be made for your handicap.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lundgren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/154820-storyteller-how-do-you-consider-attributes-under-one-dot?p=155941#post155941\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you state one way is right, and everything else is wrong, without adding the qualifier &quot;for me,&quot; I might start to argue even if your way of right happens to correspond with my own preference.</div> </div> </div> </div>Have at it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/271-jimb","name":"JimB","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar271_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-02T01:03:39-07:00","id":156309,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lundgren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/154820-storyteller-how-do-you-consider-attributes-under-one-dot?p=155841#post155841\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Over here in Sweden, we had a conscript military up to just recently, and they kept the statistics. The physical capacity of the conscripts have been in decline since the fifties, to really taking a dive the last couple of decades. So unless the two is a value relative to the current culture, the average person won't qualify for the average value of two.</div> </div> </div> </div>Keep in mind that the scale of 1-5 necessarily means that each dot represents a relatively broad spectrum of competence. It is entirely possible that the least average (current) and the highest one (during the 50s) both fall within 2 dots of strength.<br /> <br /> I mean, it is known that IQ tests consistently have to be adjusted to average at 100 because people consistently keep getting better at it. Does it mean that common people have higher mental stats now? I posit that to not be the case because the dots operate on a broad enough scale to cover a considerable range of competencies.<br /> <br /> One might also consider that 2 dots is called average not because it is statistically so, but because that word made an adequate label to put between &quot;poor&quot; and &quot;good&quot;. It covers everything from somewhat under average to somewhat above average.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:10px\">As an aside, If you consider that the label &quot;average&quot; means a rather broad average spectrum instead of a narrow statistical average, it might explain those military statistics even if their data doesn't completely fall within 2 dots: More people likely had physical primary in 50s than now, so there are a few 3s or 4s in in more quantity than 1s throwing off the statistics as compared to in-game dots XD</span><br /> <br /> The guy who can carry 30kg box with a decent posture and the guy who does it curving his back to help support the box with his body both have Strength 2 because the system is not granular enough to cover such small differences. Likewise, at least the 10 best placed or so (possibly more) Olympic athletes in the 100m dash likely have the exact same dots in their sheets regarding their ability to run. Again, the system is not granular enough to account for the milliseconds of difference.<br /> <br /> So, going back to the actual subject of the thread, given these broad values assigned for each dot, I don't think there is anything to be represented by negative dots. Zero dots cover all the things from dead to very incapacitated in various manners to needing a lot of care from someone to juuuust not capable enough for the exciting life of a WoD character. Then, for various states of poor competence but still capable, one dot (plus merits and flaws, possibly) deals with it and so on as the dots increase.<br /> <br /> I mean, keeping with Strength since it is the easiest to measure. If one dot is up to 40lbs, 2 dots is up to 100lbs, 3 dots is up to 250 lbs and so on. I don't really see it as justifiable to have a zero value of, like, 15 lbs, then -1 at 7 lbs, -2 at 3 lbs, -3 at...do you see how it becomes kinda pointless penny-counting. Especially since it mucks up the experience values and probably a host of other system math for very little gain in rather fringe verisimilitude.<br /> <br /> And I'm meandering and repeating things because if is 5:00 AM here and I should be in bed, so I'll stop now. Hopefully it is still clear enough to understand my opinion ^_^<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>You need a picture altered to fit your Exalted character, or just looking for some visual inspiration? Check out the twice-reborn <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/266-photoshop-exaltification\" target=\"_blank\"> Exaltification thread</a>. And here is my <a href=\"http://danelsan.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Deviantart page</a><br /> <br /> S\u00f8ren Kierkegaard + Kim Kardashian = <a href=\"https://twitter.com/KimKierkegaard\" target=\"_blank\">Brilliance</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/868-danelsan","name":"danelsan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar868_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1492"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-02T04:11:07-07:00","id":156373,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stupid Loserman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/154820-storyteller-how-do-you-consider-attributes-under-one-dot?p=156211#post156211\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Or in other words, two isn't a value relative to the current culture, it's a value relative to the <i>context of the game,</i> whether that's a sleepy New England town or jet-setting across Europe. If your mummy frankensteins aren't visiting Moscow then it doesn't really matter to accurately model whether they would be able to arm-wrestle your average Muscovite. Five dots is a simple spread that's versatile without requiring much thinking. Apply the scale to your game and not your game to the scale.</div> </div> </div> </div>I buy that argument.<br /> <br /> The problem for me in that case is that I seem to be incapable to keep ideas small and confined, so even if I stick the players marooned on an isolated island in the middle of the Pacific, I better mentally prepare for the possibility that the frankensteinian mummy reach Moscow (mind to fertile, aka to much crap up there <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> )<br /> <br /> Loving to tinker with rule systems isn't really helping either <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2839-lundgren","name":"Lundgren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-02T04:18:28-07:00","id":156377,"title":"","content":"Tinkering with rules is fun! When the Broken-Winged Crane book came out for Exalted I wrote a whole big old tree of powers based on that book's level of progression that I never even got to take. But often simplicity is best, and an otherwise interesting rule has to bow to the needs of playability. 1 to 5 is a really intuitive scale for people to judge things on, whereas -1 to 5 is harder to immediately grasp and work with.<br /> <br /> Now if you'll excuse me, I need to go back to planning that Russian arm-wrestling tournament adventure to run my mummy frankensteins through.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-02T04:40:05-07:00","id":156392,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JimB</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/154820-storyteller-how-do-you-consider-attributes-under-one-dot?p=156238#post156238\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My own opinion is I'll stick with what the book says, since that doesn't put me in a position where I have to invent new rules that broaden a 1-5 scale to include negative integers in order to work. That may sound like a cop-out, but you did ask.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sounds like a solid &quot;If it ain't broken, don't fix it; and it's ain't broken to me&quot; attitude <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Even if it is a &quot;not broken enough&quot;, I don't find it to be a cop-out. I wouldn't mind playing in a game under that premises, I just probably wouldn't run one that way myself (not sure anyone would notice the difference, considering how uncommon those values would be in actual play).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Below average; deficient in daily situations such that special allowances need to be made for your handicap. </div> </div> </div>Which is how I perceive the -1 and 0 value. I guess I'm just more comfortable to kind of imagine a bell curve over the range, with 2 in the middle; which put three values on either side.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Have at it. </div> </div> </div>Nah. I like the &quot;My own opinion is...&quot; too much. I guess I settle with discuss instead of argue <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2839-lundgren","name":"Lundgren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-02T06:00:19-07:00","id":156426,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>danelsan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/154820-storyteller-how-do-you-consider-attributes-under-one-dot?p=156309#post156309\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Keep in mind that the scale of 1-5 necessarily means that each dot represents a relatively broad spectrum of competence. It is entirely possible that the least average (current) and the highest one (during the 50s) both fall within 2 dots of strength.<br /> <br /> I mean, it is known that IQ tests consistently have to be adjusted to average at 100 because people consistently keep getting better at it. Does it mean that common people have higher mental stats now? I posit that to not be the case because the dots operate on a broad enough scale to cover a considerable range of competencies.<br /> <br /> One might also consider that 2 dots is called average not because it is statistically so, but because that word made an adequate label to put between &quot;poor&quot; and &quot;good&quot;. It covers everything from somewhat under average to somewhat above average.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree that it is a quote broad range for each step, and it is steps that makes a lot more sense to me than say the 100-step range of Rolemaster.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, going back to the actual subject of the thread, given these broad values assigned for each dot, I don't think there is anything to be represented by negative dots. Zero dots cover all the things from dead to very incapacitated in various manners to needing a lot of care from someone to juuuust not capable enough for the exciting life of a WoD character. Then, for various states of poor competence but still capable, one dot (plus merits and flaws, possibly) deals with it and so on as the dots increase. </div> </div> </div>Your explanation of one dot, &quot;poor competence but still capable&quot;, match quite well with how I have sorted that value into my head. Now, we might have a different opinion on what poor competence and capable actually encompasses. But I guess the main difference is in if we think it is worth the extra effort to rule-wise handle people not able to handle WoD, or would not be considered for exaltation even if doing something heroic enough.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I mean, keeping with Strength since it is the easiest to measure. If one dot is up to 40lbs, 2 dots is up to 100lbs, 3 dots is up to 250 lbs and so on. I don't really see it as justifiable to have a zero value of, like, 15 lbs, then -1 at 7 lbs, -2 at 3 lbs, -3 at...do you see how it becomes kinda pointless penny-counting. Especially since it mucks up the experience values and probably a host of other system math for very little gain in rather fringe verisimilitude. </div> </div> </div>I can see you consider it pointless penny-counting. But for me? Nope. Juggling numbers generally comes easy to me, so anything like this won't even register. I actually have more of a problem with keeping track of the merits and flaws.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And I'm meandering and repeating things because if is 5:00 AM here and I should be in bed, so I'll stop now. Hopefully it is still clear enough to understand my opinion ^_^ </div> </div> </div>Well. Either it is, or I'm just under the illusion I understand it. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2839-lundgren","name":"Lundgren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-02T07:17:49-07:00","id":156471,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stupid Loserman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/154820-storyteller-how-do-you-consider-attributes-under-one-dot?p=156377#post156377\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Tinkering with rules is fun! When the Broken-Winged Crane book came out for Exalted I wrote a whole big old tree of powers based on that book's level of progression that I never even got to take. But often simplicity is best, and an otherwise interesting rule has to bow to the needs of playability. 1 to 5 is a really intuitive scale for people to judge things on, whereas -1 to 5 is harder to immediately grasp and work with.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree 1-5 is slightly easier to grasp, but I have lost count on how many times I've seen discussions/rants elsewhere about people handling 3 as the average in the StoryTeller system, I'm not sure I agree on its intuitiveness. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Unless just using the dot system as a base for dice-less gaming, it also have quite a speed bump in the beginning. I mean, if I want to get a game up and running quickly with people not having a rule system in common, I would pick Star Wars D6, James Bond or some BRP derivate.<br /> <br /> I might be wrong, but where I perceive the strengths of the ST-system are within the speed you can play it with after you have got past that speed bump, and the different power levels you can use it for (after all, both nWoD and Exalted appears to handle a game where the player characters are just mortals quite well, where most other system would work for only one &quot;power segment&quot;). <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now if you'll excuse me, I need to go back to planning that Russian arm-wrestling tournament adventure to run my mummy frankensteins through. </div> </div> </div>Don't let them swim the Pacific first. It will take ages until they get dry again.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2839-lundgren","name":"Lundgren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-02T21:17:16-07:00","id":157430,"title":"","content":"I referred to the rules previously because I've been able to use them well and would support their being used in this case. <br /> <br /> I've never had much difficulty imagining the different thresholds and I've been able to explain them quickly to new players, so the spectrum and its intuitiveness have never been points of contention for me. 1 to 5 works about as well as the other imaginary scales in games to measure human capabilities, which is to say, it requires some imagination regardless. <br /> <br /> I would rather handle special cases regarding low attributes (due to disease, etc.) with flaws, Conditions, or other established mechanics that the system readily supports than try to tack on a system it was never intended for. I don't mind wrestling a system into a shape that makes better sense to me if it's so much of a problem that it keeps coming up in-game and continues to be a bother rather than a one-time issue. Without a practical and pressing reason, the effort itself would just not be worth it to me; there are too many other things to work on.<br /> <br /> I also don't see my players particularly enjoying a return to negative numbers in their gaming. We were done with that many years ago. It's always good to let players know about major house ruling changes you intend to use before a game starts, anyway.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kismet's <a href=\"http://www.wod.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">WOD</a> - <a href=\"http://www.dnd.kismetrose.com/\" target=\"_blank\">D&amp;D</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/BoundinDreams\" target=\"_blank\">C:tL</a> - <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TheNamelessEmpire\" target=\"_blank\">M:tC</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/227-kismetrose","name":"KismetRose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-03T04:25:34-07:00","id":157715,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>KismetRose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/154820-storyteller-how-do-you-consider-attributes-under-one-dot?p=157430#post157430\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's always good to let players know about major house ruling changes you intend to use before a game starts, anyway.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. Everything that falls under expectation management tend to be a good thing to do.<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2839-lundgren","name":"Lundgren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}